  catalog_annotations?: Record<string, string>;
  // Names the host Component attachment slot this attachment fills, or "free" when it fills no declared slot. The pairing is by name — a deployment fills a slot by attaching a Feature under the slot's name — so the value is an exact key from the resolved Component's declared slot set, never anything read out of the name itself. The slot name is carried rather than a bare boolean because which slot was filled is the more useful half of the answer. Empty when the host Component's declared slot set could not be resolved, which is distinct from "free": free is the positive finding that no declared slot carries this name.
  binding?: string;
  // Operational configuration payload for the attachment. For a credential, whichever form the author declared — a pointer into a secret store, or the material itself.
  values?: unknown;
  // Per-instance physical sizing for this attachment.
  infra?: unknown;
  // Agnostic injection routing. Maps local variables to Target provides keys.
  inject?: unknown;
  // Distinguishes attachments authored in a features: block (explicit) from synthetic attachments produced by the annotation-driven expansion pass (annotation), or mixed when the attachment's value/infra leaves carry a mix of both (per-leaf detail then lives in the provenance maps).
  origin?: string;
  // Annotation name that triggered a synthetic attachment. Empty when origin is explicit.
  annotation_key?: string;
  // Source location of the annotation declaration as <release-file-path>:<dotted-yaml-key-path>. Empty when origin is explicit.
  annotation_source?: string;
  // Per-leaf origin for the values payload, keyed by dotted leaf path.
  values_provenance?: Record<string, FieldProvenance>;
  // Per-leaf origin for the infra payload, keyed by dotted leaf path.
  infra_provenance?: Record<string, FieldProvenance>;
  // True when this attachment handles material an author declared sensitive — either the attached Feature declares a sensitive leaf in its values, infra or provides pillar, or the host Component's attachment slot of the same name declares the material it carries sensitive. Derived from those declarations over the merged attachment, never from a value's content or a name. Unset when neither declaration is present.
  sensitive?: boolean;
  // The delivery keys of inject whose referenced provides key the attached Feature declares sensitive, in inject's own shape: mechanism, then delivery key, with the referenced provides key as the value. A subset of inject. Empty when the attachment declares no delivery mechanism or references no sensitive provides key.
  sensitive_inject?: unknown;
  // The leaves this attachment authors a value at that the attached Feature declares sensitive, each as the pillar-prefixed dotted path the authored datum occupies: values.<path> or infra.<path>. This is the per-leaf unit of the sensitivity marking, and it answers a question neither of the other two does — sensitive gives one answer for the whole attachment, sensitive_inject gives one per inject delivery key, and neither names an individual authored leaf. A leaf appears only where the join is exact: an authored leaf path equal to a path the Feature's schema declares sensitive, with nothing read out of the path's own text and no value inspected. The per-attachment answer is never spread across individual leaves, which would assert something false about a leaf an author left readable. Absence therefore states that no authored leaf matched a declaration, never that nothing on this attachment carries sensitive material — a guest that reads an absent leaf as permission to display or emit a value in the clear has read it backwards. The marking is part of this payload's contract rather than a response to any one plugin: a policy that needs it can be written whenever the need arises, without the payload having to be widened first to make it expressible, so a plugin finding this key absent on an attachment learns that the attachment authors no declared-sensitive leaf and nothing about whether anything reads the key. Leaves arrive in the order the attachment authored them, the values pillar's before the infra pillar's — the same author order the values and infra payloads themselves carry, and what makes the payload byte-identical across runs of identical input. Empty when the attachment authors no leaf its Feature declares sensitive.
